    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## 1. Uniform finite-ring criterion for elementary reversibility\n\n**Conjecture.** An elementary binary cellular automaton is reversible on every finite periodic ring if and only if its rule is one of 15, 51, 85, 170, 204, and 240.\n\nThe key insight is that finite rings exhibit circumference-dependent accidental bijections, while intersecting several circumferences rapidly isolates the coordinate projections and their complements. **Why now?** The computations through circumference 12 give a stable six-rule intersection, and the shift--complement structure supplies a conceptual target for a transfer-matrix proof.\n\n## 2. Residual detection of nonreversibility\n\n**Conjecture.** Every noninjective one-dimensional cellular automaton over a finite alphabet is noninjective on some finite periodic ring, with the detecting circumference bounded effectively in terms of alphabet size and radius.\n\nThe key insight is that collisions of bi-infinite configurations should be compressible into cycles in a finite de Bruijn product graph. **Why now?** The observed accidental finite-ring bijections show that one must quantify the detecting circumference; a graph-theoretic bound would turn finite experiments into a complete decision procedure.\n\n## 3. Growth of the reversible automorphism group\n\n**Conjecture.** For every finite alphabet with at least two symbols, the group of reversible one-dimensional cellular automata contains finitely generated subgroups of exponential growth that strictly extend the shift--symbol-permutation subgroup.\n\nThe key insight is that fixed-radius strata are not groups, but bounded-radius generators can create increasingly large interaction ranges under composition. **Why now?** The infinite shift--complement subgroup identifies the first algebraic layer; controlled marker constructions offer a route to noncommuting reversible generators beyond it.\n\n## 4. A Galois connection between radius and observable partitions\n\n**Conjecture.** Radius-bounded local dynamics and translation-invariant finite-window equivalence relations form a Galois connection whose closed elements classify the information visible to reversible cellular automata at each scale.\n\nThe key insight is that enlarging an observation window refines configuration partitions, while enlarging rule radius expands the transformations that preserve those observations. **Why now?** Fiber bounds on finite periodic systems and the failure of fixed-radius closure point to order-theoretic closure, rather than a finite symmetric group, as the appropriate replacement structure.\n\n## 5. Quantitative inverse-radius bounds\n\n**Conjecture.** There is no polynomial bound, uniform over alphabet size, relating the radius of a reversible cellular automaton to the minimum radius of its inverse.\n\nThe key insight is that bijectivity guarantees a local inverse but need not preserve locality at a comparable scale; information can be routed over long distances before being decoded. **Why now?** Separating global group structure from fixed-radius strata makes inverse-radius distortion a precise quantitative invariant of reversible computation.\n",
