    "description": "# Future Directions: Algebraic Geometry of Neural Decision Boundaries\n\n## 1. Signed tropical-rational normal form\n\n**Conjecture.** Every scalar feed-forward ReLU network admits a depth-preserving representation as a difference of two max-affine expressions, with the number of leaves bounded singly exponentially in depth and polynomially in the product of layer widths. Its decision boundary is therefore an equality locus of two tropical polynomials rather than, in general, a single tropical hypersurface.\n\nThe key insight is that negative output weights destroy convex max-affine structure but suggest a tropical-rational replacement closed under subtraction. **Why now?** The depth-controlled max-affine analysis isolates exactly which step fails for signed networks and supplies the analytic estimate needed on each side of the proposed normal form.\n\n## 2. Margin-stable dequantized decision topology\n\n**Conjecture.** On every compact domain where a max-affine classifier has no critical value in a band of width `d log(2)/\u03b2` around zero, its tropical decision set and log-sum-exp decision set are ambient isotopic.\n\nThe key insight is that uniform value control preserves labels outside a narrow band, while a critical-value exclusion should upgrade label stability to topological stability. **Why now?** A sharp depth-dependent error bound is available; the remaining test is geometric transversality within the explicit uncertainty band.\n\n## 3. Arrangement-sensitive singularity bound\n\n**Conjecture.** For max-affine expressions with affine leaves in general position in dimension `n`, the codimension-two singular strata of the decision complex are bounded by the number of intersecting `(n+1)`-subsets of active leaves, rather than by a product of pair counts across network layers.\n\nThe key insight is that singularities arise from simultaneous affine ties in the realized upper envelope, not merely from pairs of neurons in an architecture. **Why now?** The width-one obstruction rules out the unguarded layerwise pair-product formula and points to active-set incidence as the correct combinatorial invariant.\n\n## 4. Realized-region recurrence with activation rank\n\n**Conjecture.** A layerwise region recurrence becomes valid after replacing raw width `w_i` by the rank of the affine hyperplane arrangement induced on each incoming region; the resulting bound is the product of arrangement growth functions and is strictly sharper than `2^L \u220f w_i` whenever bottlenecks occur.\n\nThe key insight is that width counts available gates, whereas arrangement rank counts gates capable of cutting a specific inherited region. **Why now?** The exponential-product expression has been separated from its unproved geometric recurrence, making the missing local cutting hypothesis explicit and experimentally testable.\n\n## 5. Tropical VC bound through active-set compression\n\n**Conjecture.** For fixed input dimension, binary classifiers represented as differences of two depth-`L` max-affine expressions with `N` total leaves have VC dimension `O(n L log N)`, with matching lower bounds for generic leaf arrangements.\n\nThe key insight is that label patterns should be encoded by active-leaf certificates along a depth hierarchy, linking tropical cell combinatorics to statistical shattering. **Why now?** The max-affine syntax supplies a canonical certificate tree, and the catalogued VC compactness framework supplies a natural target for turning certificate counts into dimension bounds.\n",
