    "description": "# Future Directions for Practical `k`-Copwin Recognition\n\n## 1. Symmetry-reduced backward search\n\n**Conjecture.** For every finite graph and every positive `k`, quotienting cop configurations by permutations of the `k` cops preserves every bounded-horizon winning region and the stabilized winning region, while reducing the configuration factor from `|V|^k` to the number of multisets of size `k` drawn from `V`.\n\nThe key insight is that capture, simultaneous cop movement, and the cops-first forcing condition are invariant under relabeling indistinguishable cops. **Why now?** The iteration invariant isolates exactly the semantic facts that a quotient implementation must preserve, making a correctness proof and an empirical complexity comparison sharply testable.\n\n## 2. Certified finite stabilization bound\n\n**Conjecture.** On a graph with `n` vertices and `k` cops, backward search stabilizes after at most `n^(k+1)` strict updates; moreover, there are families for which the necessary number of updates grows polynomially in the number of game states.\n\nThe key insight is that the winning tables form an increasing chain in a finite state space, so every non-stable update must add a state. **Why now?** The established monotonicity and fixed-point characterization reduce termination to a quantitative chain-length problem, and implementations can record the exact stabilization depth across graph families.\n\n## 3. Rank functions as capture-time certificates\n\n**Conjecture.** Every state in the stabilized winning region has a least iteration rank, and this rank equals the minimum worst-case capture time among all cops' strategies from that state.\n\nThe key insight is that the first table containing a state records the outer existential choice of a cops' move followed by the worst robber response, which is precisely a minimax recurrence. **Why now?** Bounded-horizon correctness already gives one direction; extracting least ranks would turn a yes/no recognizer into a strategy and optimal-capture-time certificate generator.\n\n## 4. Kernel and attractor duality for finite pursuit games\n\n**Conjecture.** After enriching positions with whose turn it is, the complement of the stabilized cops' attractor is the unique kernel of the well-founded game obtained by ranking all winning positions and restricting moves to strictly decreasing rank.\n\nThe key insight is that backward attractors describe winning positions while kernels describe losing positions, and a capture-rank orientation removes cyclic play from the winning subgame. **Why now?** The two theories now coexist with compatible relational formulations, so the proposed bridge can be tested first on paths, cycles, trees, and complete graphs before seeking a general proof.\n\n## 5. Structural compression on bounded-width graphs\n\n**Conjecture.** For each fixed `k` and treewidth bound `t`, the stabilized `k`-copwin computation admits a representation whose size is linear in the number of vertices, with constants depending only on `k` and `t`.\n\nThe key insight is that the one-round forcing operator only observes local adjacency and membership information, suggesting a finite interface state over each tree-decomposition bag. **Why now?** The semantic operator is independent of any concrete table layout, enabling alternative compressed implementations to be compared against the same correctness specification.\n",
